//! Entries slice — Batch 210 (Plan 210) — the single 10-08 late-envelope
//! lead: one `ggml-org/llama.cpp` commit (MIT; the full `.patch` read at
//! authoring from
//! `https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/commit/d6cf9acb….patch` — the
//! patch-at-pin read is the verification source, no ephemeral clone
//! created). Quotes verified VERBATIM against the patch.
//!
//! Pack composition (category split D +1; corpus 776 → 777):
//! - D +1 `expert-cache-write-path-regime-gated` (d6cf9acb #29887 — the
//!   `MoE` expert GPU cache whose WRITE path is regime-gated: small
//!   batches plan-and-evict, large batches read-if-present and never
//!   touch the LRU, so a prefill-sized plan cannot evict the
//!   generation working set; the cache-update hook rides the
//!   scheduler's own host-weight split boundary — no new sync point)
//!
//! The same envelope pass's other llama.cpp commit (`50a6c5cf` #30062,
//! mtmd cohere2 vision) is model plumbing — off-lane, watch-only.
//! cubecl (`2f0d8e8df5`) and bevy (`5cd69c1357`) UNCHANGED since the
//! Batch 209 poll.
//!
//! DEDUP (run at authoring, the named vocabularies over
//! `src/domains/kernel_opt/corpus/`):
//! - `lru / slot map / expert cache / moe cache / bank / evict /
//!   working set / resident` — the B94 family (jundot/omlx PR #3260)
//!   owns the victim POLICY (`decayed-frequency-eviction-recency-
//!   tiebreak-route-protection`), the one-shot REGION isolation
//!   (`scratch-bank-isolates-one-shot-operands-from-hot-cache`), and
//!   the regime-START admission (`fill-gate-speculation-until-working-
//!   set-resident`); `host-cache-immutable-device-weights` (B26) caches
//!   device weights on the HOST — the inverse direction. The
//!   WRITE-PATH regime gate is owned by nobody; distinct-from'd in-rule
//!   against all four.
//! - `prefix-cache-entry-one-token-early` (B200) is a KV/prefix cache —
//!   a different operand class. `graph-pool-handle-fresh-per-generation`
//!   (B200) is allocator pool identity. No collision.

use crate::domains::kernel_opt::KernelRule;

/// Data table, not logic — grows with distillation batches.
pub(super) fn entries() -> Vec<KernelRule> {
    vec![
        // ── D: serving/scheduling (#29887) — the expert GPU cache whose
        // write path is regime-gated; the update hook rides the
        // scheduler's host-weight split; large batches read-only.
        KernelRule {
            rule_id: "expert-cache-write-path-regime-gated".to_string(),
            gpu_concept: "A device-side expert cache shared by NARROW (decode/generation) and WIDE (prefill/large-ubatch) regimes must gate its WRITE path on the regime — plan-and-evict ONLY for batches whose working set fits the slots; wider batches read the cache if-present but NEVER touch the LRU, so a prefill-sized plan cannot evict the generation working set. THE WRITE-PATH LAW, verbatim (llama-moe-cache.cpp): '// large batches use most experts of a layer, so they gain little from the cache and would evict the experts used in generation' — the graph-side arm declines the cache for them (get_slot_map returns nullptr when 'n_tokens > max_batch || std::min(n_tokens*n_expert_used, l.slot_map->ne[1]) > groups[l.ig].n_slots', max_batch = 32) while the copy path still READS it ('// large batches only read the cache, so the experts used in generation stay in it' — copy_experts copies an expert out of the bank when its slot is live and returns 0 otherwise; the misses upload through the ordinary copy path WITHOUT entering the bank). This is scratch-isolation WITHOUT a second region: the same goal as scratch-bank-isolates-one-shot-operands-from-hot-cache achieved by denying the write path during the wide regime rather than routing cold operands to a separate scratch bank. THE FIT-GATED PLAN: the plan itself refuses loud when one call's distinct experts exceed the slots ('if (uniq.size() > (size_t) n_slots) { return false; }' — '// give a slot to each expert selected by ids in layer il, the misses evict the least recently used experts // returns false if the ids select more distinct experts than there are slots'). THE SEAM (the second law — no existing rule owns it): the cache-update hook rides a split boundary the scheduler ALREADY creates — the per-layer slot map is allocated as a HOST weight ('// the slot map is a host weight, so the scheduler starts a new split here and copies it with the copy callback // the callback reads the selected experts, uploads the missing ones and updates the slot map'), so the get_rows that reads it forces the scheduler's host-weight split and the existing copy callback runs the plan there; NO new synchronization point is added — the cache piggybacks on a boundary the operand placement already pays for. THE ORDERING LAW within one plan: hits are touched to the LRU tail BEFORE the miss pass evicts ('// hits go to the tail first, so the head can be evicted below') — a hit in the same call never becomes its own plan's eviction victim. THE UPLOAD-SHAPE law: misses are SORTED by expert id and consecutive (expert, slot) runs merge into single async copies ('// sorted misses usually get consecutive slots, so the uploads can be merged'); the read side detects the same runs ('while (e + n <= last && slots[e + n] == slots[e] + n) { n++; }'). ALLOCATION face: the bank carries 'n_slots + 1' slots ('// one extra slot at the end, CUDA MMQ can read past the last expert'). BUDGET face: 'split the budget by the size of the experts, so each group caches the same fraction of its experts' — same-layout layers share banks and one LRU group. CORRECTNESS PIN: a forced-eviction test asserts logits vs CPU ('// the cache has only a few slots (4 for 288 KiB experts), so the experts are evicted and uploaded again' — 1536 KiB budget, n_ubatch = 2, NMSE-gated). REGIME: expert/adapter weight caches serving mixed narrow+wide traffic where the narrow regime's working set is the one worth protecting. ANTI-REGIME: single-regime caches (no split to gate); caches whose wide regime IS the hot consumer. Distinct from decayed-frequency-eviction-recency-tiebreak-route-protection (the VICTIM policy inside a plan — decayed frequency vs plain LRU; this rule decides WHETHER a plan runs at all and which regime may write), from scratch-bank-isolates-one-shot-operands-from-hot-cache (region isolation for one-shot operands; this is write-path denial for the whole wide regime, no second region, and the wide regime still benefits from READS), from fill-gate-speculation-until-working-set-resident (gates a SPECULATIVE regime on fill state; this gates per-call cache PLANS on working-set fit), and from host-cache-immutable-device-weights (B26 — device weights cached on the HOST; the inverse direction of host-resident weights cached on the DEVICE). NotBenchable here (no in-source perf numbers; the NMSE correctness test + teardown hit-rate logs are the evidence). CITE: ggml-org/llama.cpp @ d6cf9acb25e6c657d6c6a4645ab7b69fce119995 (#29887, MIT) — src/llama-moe-cache.cpp (the moe_cache_lru plan: the fit refusal, the hit-first touch pass, the sorted-merge uploads; impl::get_slot_map: the max_batch = 32 + fit decline; impl::copy_experts: the read-only large-batch arm; the slot-map-as-host-weight seam in build_moe_cache_slots' comment), src/llama-graph.cpp (build_moe_cache_slots), tests/test-llama-archs.cpp (the forced-eviction MoE-cache device config). Batch 210 / Plan 210.".to_string(),
            ref_shape: "// naive: one LRU bank serves every regime — a prefill-sized batch\n// plans for its whole working set, evicting exactly the experts the\n// next decode token needs; the decode hit rate collapses after every\n// wide call and the uploads re-pay what the eviction threw away.\nstruct ExpertCache {\n    slots: Vec<Option<(usize, u32)>>, // (layer, expert) per slot\n    lru: VecDeque<usize>,             // head = least recently used\n}\n\nimpl ExpertCache {\n    fn plan_naive(&mut self, layer: usize, ids: &[u32]) {\n        let uniq = unique(ids);\n        for e in uniq {\n            if !self.holds(layer, e) {\n                let victim = self.lru.pop_front().expect(\"empty\");\n                self.evict(victim); // a decode-hot expert may be the victim\n                self.upload_and_fill(layer, e); // sync point per miss\n            }\n            self.touch(layer, e); // hits and misses interleave\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn holds(&self, _layer: usize, _e: u32) -> bool {\n        false\n    }\n    fn evict(&mut self, _slot: usize) {}\n    fn upload_and_fill(&mut self, _layer: usize, _e: u32) {}\n    fn touch(&mut self, _layer: usize, _e: u32) {}\n}\n\nfn unique(ids: &[u32]) -> Vec<u32> {\n    ids.to_vec()\n}".to_string(),
            opt_pattern: "// regime-gated: the WRITE path runs only when the working set fits;\n// wide batches read-if-present and never touch the LRU; the plan hook\n// rides the scheduler's host-weight split (no new sync point); hits\n// touch before misses evict; misses sort + merge into run uploads.\nconst MAX_BATCH: usize = 32; // 'large batches use most experts of a\n                             // layer, so they gain little from the\n                             // cache and would evict the experts used\n                             // in generation'\n\nstruct GatedCache {\n    n_slots: usize,\n    slots: Vec<i32>,              // expert -> slot, -1 = not cached\n    lru_head: usize,              // least recently used\n    host_slot_map_weight: HostWeight, // a HOST weight: reading it in the\n                                      // graph forces the scheduler's\n                                      // split — the copy callback IS the\n                                      // plan hook (no new sync point)\n}\n\nstruct HostWeight;\n\nimpl GatedCache {\n    // graph-side admission: declines the cache for regimes that must\n    // not write (the caller falls back to the ordinary expert path)\n    fn admits(&self, n_tokens: usize, n_expert_used: usize, n_expert: usize) -> bool {\n        n_tokens > 0\n            && n_tokens <= MAX_BATCH\n            && (n_tokens * n_expert_used).min(n_expert) <= self.n_slots\n    }\n\n    // the plan (small batches only): fit-gated, hit-first, merged runs\n    fn plan(&mut self, ids: &[u32]) -> Result<Vec<(u32, i32)>, PlanRefused> {\n        let mut uniq = unique_sorted(ids);\n        if uniq.len() > self.n_slots {\n            return Err(PlanRefused); // 'the ids select more distinct\n                                     // experts than there are slots'\n        }\n        // hits go to the tail first, so the head can be evicted below\n        let mut fills = vec![];\n        for &e in &uniq {\n            if self.slots[e as usize] >= 0 {\n                self.touch_tail(self.slots[e as usize]);\n            }\n        }\n        // sorted misses usually get consecutive slots, so the uploads\n        // can be merged (one async copy per run)\n        for (i, &e) in uniq.iter().enumerate() {\n            if self.slots[e as usize] < 0 {\n                let slot = self.evict_head_and_fill(e as usize);\n                let _ = i;\n                fills.push((e, slot));\n            }\n        }\n        Ok(merge_consecutive_runs(&fills))\n    }\n\n    // wide batches: READ only — copy out what is cached, never plan\n    fn copy_experts_readonly(&self, e: u32, last: u32) -> usize {\n        if self.slots[e as usize] < 0 {\n            return 0; // the miss uploads through the ordinary path\n        }\n        let mut n = 1;\n        while e + (n as u32) <= last\n            && self.slots[(e + n as u32) as usize] == self.slots[e as usize] + n as i32\n        {\n            n += 1; // the same run detection merges the read side too\n        }\n        n\n    }\n\n    fn touch_tail(&mut self, _slot: i32) {}\n    fn evict_head_and_fill(&mut self, _e: usize) -> i32 {\n        0\n    }\n}\n\nstruct PlanRefused;\n\nfn unique_sorted(ids: &[u32]) -> Vec<u32> {\n    let mut v = ids.to_vec();\n    v.sort_unstable();\n    v.dedup();\n    v\n}\n\nfn merge_consecutive_runs(fills: &[(u32, i32)]) -> Vec<(u32, i32)> {\n    fills.to_vec()\n}".to_string(),
        },
    ]
}
